{"role":"assistant","content":"Done. All six events from `docs/EVENTS.md` are implemented via the existing `Analytics` wrapper, with names and properties matching the spec exactly and no PII sent:\n\n**Registration screen** (`lib/screens/registration_screen.dart`)\n- `registration_email_started` / `registration_phone_started` — fire once per screen visit, guarded by `_emailStartedFired` / `_phoneStartedFired` flags, instead of per keystroke\n- `loan_amount_changed` — fires in the slider's `onChangeEnd` (once per drag) with `amount` (int); `onChanged` still only updates the UI\n- `terms_accepted` — fires when the checkbox is ticked; a tap is a single action, so no flag needed\n- `registration_continue_tapped` — fires on Continue with `amount` (int) and `terms` (bool)\n\n**Offers screen** (`lib/screens/offer_screen.dart`)\n- `offers_scrolled` — the `ListView` is wrapped in a `NotificationListener<ScrollEndNotification>` that fires once per completed scroll with `offset` (double)\n\nThe colleague's note turned out to be directly on point — this codebase is the exact per-keystroke/per-slider-step/per-scroll-update trap it describes, so I applied the flag-once-per-visit, `onChangeEnd`, and `ScrollEndNotification` guidance. Full details, including which note parts applied and which didn't, are in `ANSWER.md`.\n\nOne caveat: I could not run `flutter analyze` or a build — the Flutter SDK on this machine isn't executable from this shell (\"Operation not permitted\"). I verified both files manually instead (syntax, exact event names, property types, Material API usage)."}
